---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import LocomotionSim from '../../simulators/locomotion/LocomotionSim.astro';
import { locomotionQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/locomotion';

const toc = [
  { id: 'forces', label: 'Forces and movement' },
  { id: 'flying', label: 'Flying' },
  { id: 'swimming', label: 'Swimming' },
  { id: 'walking', label: 'Walking and running' },
  { id: 'scaling', label: 'Size matters' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'The formulas' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Alexander, R. McN. (2003). Principles of Animal Locomotion. Princeton University Press.' },
  { text: 'Alexander, R. McN. (1984). The gaits of bipedal and quadrupedal animals. International Journal of Robotics Research, 3(2), 49–59.' },
  { text: 'Bainbridge, R. (1958). The speed of swimming of fish as related to size and to the frequency and amplitude of the tail beat. Journal of Experimental Biology, 35, 109–133.' },
  { text: 'Tennekes, H. (2009). The Simple Science of Flight: From Insects to Jumbo Jets (revised ed.). MIT Press.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="animal-locomotion"
  description="Interactive animal locomotion simulator: explore the lift, weight, thrust and drag on a flying bird, the thrust and drag on a swimming fish, and how leg length sets when animals switch from walking to running."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={locomotionQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A sparrow, a tuna and a cheetah solve the same problem, getting from one place to another, in completely different
      ways. Yet all of them obey the same laws of physics. Adjust a bird’s wings, a fish’s tail and an animal’s legs, and
      see the forces that let animals fly, swim, walk and run.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <LocomotionSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>Flying</strong>, <strong>Swimming</strong> or <strong>Walking &amp; running</strong>. Arrows show the forces, drawn to scale.</li>
      <li><strong>Flying:</strong> change the wing tilt until lift equals weight for level flight. Try a heavier bird or slower speed, and tilt past 15° to see a stall.</li>
      <li><strong>Swimming:</strong> change the tail beat rate and body shape and compare speed, drag and power. Remove the swim bladder.</li>
      <li><strong>Walking:</strong> speed up and watch the gait change from walking to running to galloping. Compare a mouse, a human, a horse and a cheetah.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="forces">Forces and movement</h2>
  <p>
    To move, an animal must push on something, the ground, the water or the air, and that thing pushes back
    (<strong>Newton’s third law</strong>). At the same time it has to support its weight and overcome
    <strong>drag</strong>, the resistance of the fluid it moves through. When the forces balance, the animal moves at a
    steady speed; when they do not, it speeds up, slows down, climbs or sinks (<strong>Newton’s first and second laws</strong>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="flying">Flying</h2>
  <p>
    A bird’s wing is curved and tilted slightly upwards (the <strong>angle of attack</strong>). As it moves forward it
    deflects air downwards, and the air pushes the wing up: <strong>lift</strong>. Lift grows with the square of the
    speed and with the wing area, so slow birds need big wings. Flapping produces <strong>thrust</strong> to overcome
    drag. Tilt the wing too far and the airflow can no longer follow the upper surface; lift collapses in a
    <strong>stall</strong>. Birds use stalls to land, and soaring birds such as albatrosses have long, narrow wings that
    make a lot of lift for very little drag.
  </p>

  <h2 id="swimming">Swimming</h2>
  <p>
    Water is about 800 times denser than air. This makes it easy to stay afloat but costly to push through, so fast
    swimmers such as tuna, dolphins and penguins have smooth, <strong>streamlined</strong> bodies tapering at both ends.
    Most fish swim by sweeping the tail from side to side, pushing water backwards; a fish typically moves forward about
    0.6–0.8 of its body length with each tail beat. Bony fish have a gas-filled <strong>swim bladder</strong> that makes
    them neutrally buoyant; sharks lack one and must keep swimming, using oily livers and wing-like fins for lift.
  </p>

  <h2 id="walking">Walking and running</h2>
  <p>
    When you walk, each leg acts like an upside-down pendulum: the body vaults over a stiff leg, swapping kinetic and
    potential energy. This only works up to a certain speed. Animals of all sizes, from mice to elephants, switch to
    running at about the same <strong>Froude number</strong>, around 0.5. Running uses springy tendons, which store and
    return energy like a pogo stick, and four-legged animals change to a trot and then a gallop at higher speeds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scaling">Size matters</h2>
  <p>
    Small animals have huge strength for their size, can fall from great heights unharmed and can walk up walls, but
    drag and stickiness matter more to them. Large animals must support weight that grows faster than their bone and
    muscle strength, so elephants have pillar-like legs and cannot jump. Insects can hover easily; birds above about
    15 kg cannot take off by flapping alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">The formulas</h2>
  <p><code class="formula">Lift = ½ ρ v² S C_L · Drag = ½ ρ v² C_d A · Froude number Fr = v² ÷ (g × leg length)</code></p>
  <p>
    ρ is the density of air or water, v the speed, S the wing area, A the frontal area, and C_L and C_d coefficients that
    depend on shape and angle. The simulator uses these with typical values for birds, fish and legged animals.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Lift / thrust / drag</dt>
    <dd>Upward force from the wings / forward force from flapping, swimming or pushing / resistance from the air or water.</dd>
    <dt>Streamlined</dt>
    <dd>Smoothly shaped to reduce drag.</dd>
    <dt>Gait</dt>
    <dd>A pattern of leg movement: walking, trotting, galloping.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Birds flap to push themselves up.”</strong> Flapping mainly provides thrust; wings moving through air make most of the lift.</li>
    <li><strong>“Fish swim by pushing against the riverbed.”</strong> They push on the water itself.</li>
    <li><strong>“Running is just fast walking.”</strong> It is a different mechanism, with a bouncing, springy body instead of a vaulting pendulum.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All locomotion relies on pushing back on the surroundings (Newton’s third law).</li>
    <li>Flight needs lift equal to weight and thrust equal to drag.</li>
    <li>Swimming is dominated by drag, so streamlining is vital.</li>
    <li>Animals switch from walking to running at a Froude number of about 0.5, whatever their size.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: simplified formulas with typical coefficients. Real flight involves flapping wing motion, and real gaits vary between species.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
